# See-saw Test - BONANZA's SEE-SAW test course (SPEC.md 128.42), built only by the BZTILT=1 knob
# (make BUILD=<tree> BZTILT=1 bonanzadisk).  NOT a shipped course: the knob adds it to BZ_COURSES and makes it
# the pathway's pad 0, so a new game rests on it (title, Play Game, Enter).  Not on the TOUR.  A see-saw is a
# thin plate on a fulcrum that TIPS under the ball's weight: rolled onto one half, that half goes down - steel
# tips it at once, rubber after a moment, glass (light) only after about half a second - and the plate carries
# the ball with it.
#
# START is a plate at 2 units with a pad for each ball.  From it:
#  - THE LEDGE (east): a 4-cell see-saw resting with its near end down at the start plate's 2 units and its far end
#    up at 4, beside a LEDGE at 3.5 units and its goal (exit 1).  Roll up it: as soon as the ball is past the pivot
#    its weight starts to tip the plate down onto the far side.  Steel and rubber tip it before they reach the top,
#    and the ledge is out of reach; GLASS is light enough to roll off the top onto the ledge first;
#  - THE GATE (south): a 4-cell see-saw resting with its far end UP, 2 units over the far plate - a wall to roll at.
#    Roll on and up: past the pivot the plate tips and lays its far end down at the far plate's height, the ball
#    carried down with it, and on to the goal (exit 0).  Glass, slow to tip it, may run off the top first and fall;
#  - THE ROCKER (west): a 2-cell see-saw across a gap to a dead-end plate, resting tilted: every ball rocks it.
#  - THE CLOCKWORK (north, then a long way east, out of the weight plates' windows: SPEC.md 128.42.11): TIMED
#    see-saws, which ignore the ball's weight and swing on a timer (a fulcrum banded black).  THE LIFT: a plate
#    whose near end is level with the hub at rest; roll down it to its far end, against the ledge's wall, and wait -
#    the plate swings and LIFTS the ball to the ledge's height; roll off onto the ledge and its goal (exit 0).
#    THE SWING: a plate across to a dead-end pad, level only while it passes FLAT - time the crossing.
course TILT "See-saw Test"
width 128
origin 384
ball rubber
uses seesaw

floor rect 6 9 12 15 flat 2                   # the start plate
pad rubber 7 13
pad steel 8 13
pad glass 9 13
start 7 10

# THE LEDGE: the plate x 12 - 15, y 10 - 11, its pivot at 3 units (rest: the west end down at 2, the east end at 4);
# the ledge x 16 - 19 at 3.5 units
seesaw 12 10 16 11 along x pivot 3
floor rect 16 9 20 13 flat 3.5
goal tile 18 10 flat 3.5 exit 1

# THE GATE: the plate x 7 - 8, y 15 - 18, its pivot at 3 units (rest: the north end down at 2, the south end at 4);
# the far plate at 2 units and the goal
seesaw 8 15 9 19 along y pivot 3
floor rect 6 19 12 24 flat 2
goal tile 8 22 flat 2

# THE ROCKER: the plate x 4 - 5, y 11 - 12, its pivot at 2 units (rest: the east end down at 1.5), the far plate
# x 3 at 2 units (a dead end)
seesaw 4 11 6 13 along x pivot 2 rest +
floor rect 3 10 4 14 flat 2

# THE CLOCKWORK: a corridor east off the GATE's far plate to the hub x 26 - 31, y 20 - 25 at 2 units
floor rect 12 21 26 23 flat 2
floor rect 26 20 32 26 flat 2
# THE LIFT: x 28, y 26 - 29 along y, its pivot at 1 unit, resting with its PLUS end down (its near end level
# with the hub, its far end 2 units under the ledge); 128 steps a swing, 16 of each half FLAT
seesaw 28 26 29 30 along y pivot 1 rest + period 128 flat 16
floor rect 27 30 31 34 flat 1.5
goal tile 28 32 flat 1.5
# THE SWING: x 32 - 35, y 22 along x, its pivot at 2 units (FLAT: level with the hub and the pad), a quarter
# period on from the lift
seesaw 32 22 36 23 along x pivot 2 period 128 flat 24 seed 32
floor rect 36 21 39 24 flat 2

target 60
way 7 10
way 13 10
way 8 22
